@@ -3,3 +3,40 @@
 A small TypeScript library, with no runtime dependency, of building blocks for our services that store,
 exchange and update JSON: configuration files, API payloads and stored records. It is imported by the
 other projects of the team, so its behaviour must follow the published standards it implements.
+
+## Development
+
+Node.js 22.18 or later runs the TypeScript sources directly (type stripping), so no build step is
+needed to test.
+
+- `npm test` runs the test suite (`test/*.test.ts`).
+- `npm run typecheck` type-checks `src/`.
+- `npm run build` emits JavaScript and declarations to `dist/`.
+
+## Features
+
+### JSON Pointer (RFC 6901)
+
+`resolvePointer(document, pointer)` returns the value of `document` that the JSON Pointer `pointer`
+refers to. It returns the value itself, not a copy.
+
+```ts
+import { resolvePointer } from "json-toolkit";
+
+resolvePointer({ foo: ["bar", "baz"], "a/b": 1 }, "/foo/1"); // "baz"
+resolvePointer({ foo: ["bar", "baz"], "a/b": 1 }, "/a~1b"); // 1 ("~1" escapes "/", "~0" escapes "~")
+resolvePointer({ foo: 1 }, ""); // the whole document
+```
+
+It throws a `JsonPointerError` (whose `pointer` property holds the failing pointer) when:
+
+- the pointer is not valid: not a string, non-empty without a leading `/`, or a `~` not followed by
+  `0` or `1`;
+- the pointer does not resolve: a missing object member, an array index that is out of range, has a
+  leading zero or is not a number (including `-`, which RFC 6901 defines as the nonexistent element
+  after the last one), or a step into a string, number, boolean or `null`.
+
+Only the string representation is accepted; decode a URI fragment (`#/foo`) before passing it.
+`parsePointer(pointer)` is also exported: it validates a pointer and returns its unescaped reference
+tokens (`"/a~1b/0"` gives `["a/b", "0"]`). See [ADR 0001](docs/adr/0001-json-pointer.md) for the
+design decisions.
